Our dataset, "Clothing", is the sales for a clothing retailer. It contains a random sample of 60 observations of customers from a large clothing retailer. It contains the variables of the net dollar amount spent in the latest purchase, number of months since the last purchase, number of purchases in the last 12 and 24 months, dollar amount of purchases in the last 12 and 24 months, and whether they have a private label credit card with the retailer.

Our Research question is: 

Does having a private label credit card with the retailer affect frequency and money spent? (Note: Can we
track credit cards and non credit cards for a better predictor between the two?)
How does frequency, and dollar amount spent affect the most recent purchase amount for a shopper?

???Findings and why is it useful???


  From the given datasets, our group selected the Clothing data set from Stat2Data, which contains 60 observations of 8 variables, collected from a large clothing retailer. This clothing retailer offers a store credit card. Our given data records the use of these cards, as well as variables on frequency, recency of latest purchase, and money spent in the past 12 and 24 months. In this, we would like to purpose the question of how much a customer would spend in their next purchase, based off of the usage of these private-label cards? 

```{r}

rmCloth <- myClothes[1:59,]

mlr.model <- lm(Dollar24 ~ Freq24 + Card, data = rmCloth)
summary(mlr.model)
mlr.conf <- augment(mlr.model, rmCloth, interval = 'confidence')

```
